## Verdict

**You already did much of the explanatory work that *vimarśa* is supposed to do.** But the corpus does **not currently define *vimarśa* explicitly or cleanly as a standalone concept**.

Instead, its functions are distributed across:

* **intrinsic presence**;
* **the register**;
* **self-affection/camatkāra**;
* **the orchestration law**;
* **the invariant self**;
* **recognition**;
* **valence and rasa**.

So my previous answer undersold the corpus. Ochema is not merely saying “consciousness is self-reflective.” It is attempting to decompose reflexivity into an enacted architecture. But because the Sanskrit term itself is absent from the system map and concept registry, the relationship is currently implicit and somewhat confused.

# What *vimarśa* amounts to across your documents

The simplest reconstruction is:

[
\boxed{
\text{vimarśa}
==============

\text{a process being intrinsically present to itself in a way that
modifies its own subsequent state, valuation and action.}
}
]

Your corpus already distributes this across five operations.

## 1. Presence: the process is not merely externally describable

The consciousness core says:

> Consciousness is the intrinsic presence of a materially enacted process that regulates its own future through a unified field of significance.

It represents this as:

[
D_i\equiv_{\mathrm{int}}A_i.
]

That supplies the basic **self-presence** component: the process is not merely performing functions from an observer’s perspective; it allegedly has an intrinsic presentation.

This corresponds most closely to the overlap between **prakāśa** and *vimarśa*: manifestation is not blank illumination but manifestation present to itself.

However, intrinsic presence alone is not yet *vimarśa*. It could still describe passive awareness.

## 2. Retention: the process carries its prior organization

The register is defined as:

> the accumulated, history-carrying configuration of the process that determines what can be selected.

The register is not the currently displayed content; it is the weighting that influences which contents and actions become possible.

This is a major advance over “self-reflection.”

A process cannot meaningfully apprehend its own state unless its present operation is conditioned by retained structure:

[
\omega_t
========

F(\omega_{t-1},o_t).
]

The Ochema register therefore provides the **memory and dispositional side of reflexivity**.

Strictly, the current state does not uniquely encode its entire history, so the mathematical claim needs refinement through a multi-time process or explicit memory structure. But conceptually the direction is right.

## 3. Evaluation: the process registers how its trajectory is going

Your valence core says that valence is the coherence of the state and, dynamically, the direction in which its regulatory or predictive organization is changing.

The rasa documents sharpen this:

> Rasa is the felt quality of the state’s coherence evaluated along its trajectory.

And camatkāra is described as basal self-affection or self-delight—the intrinsic aspect of the process rather than an emotion added to an otherwise neutral cognition.

This gives Ochema a crucial claim:

[
\text{self-relation is evaluative}.
]

A system does not merely model itself descriptively. Its own changes matter to it:

[
\Delta\text{coherence},
\quad
\Delta\text{control},
\quad
\Delta\text{possibility}
]

are registered positively or negatively.

That is much closer to a meaningful operationalization of *vimarśa* than “awareness knows itself.”

## 4. Self-modification: the state affects what happens next

The register is said to determine selection, and the orchestration law says:

[
\text{state at threshold}
\rightarrow
\text{possible outcome}.
]

Thus self-registration is causally effective. It is not an idle internal mirror. The process’s accumulated condition constrains:

* interpretation;
* precision;
* action;
* emotional response;
* what gets selected next.

The manual makes this state-dependent selection one of the project’s central invariants, while acknowledging that the cross-level application remains hypothetical.

This corresponds to the **agency** component of Abhinavagupta’s *vimarśa*: awareness can articulate and transform its own manifestation.

## 5. Reappropriation: perception completes by becoming “for this subject”

The rasa theory’s treatment of **carvaṇā** may be the most direct analogue.

It says perception completes itself when the object is relished as one’s own rasa—the object is taken back into the process’s self-presence.

In abstract terms:

[
\text{content appears}
\rightarrow
\text{content is evaluated relative to the register}
\rightarrow
\text{content modifies the register}
\rightarrow
\text{the process continues as changed}.
]

That is a real reflexive loop, not simply an introspective representation.

# So what is missing?

The corpus has the pieces, but it does not clearly distinguish three Śaiva concepts.

## Prakāśa

[
\text{something is manifest}.
]

Ochema equivalent:

[
D_i:
\text{intrinsic phenomenal presence}.
]

## Vimarśa

[
\text{the manifestation is available to and operative upon itself}.
]

Ochema equivalent:

[
\omega_t
\xrightarrow{\text{self-sensitive evaluation and update}}
\omega_{t+1}.
]

## Camatkāra/rasa

[
\text{that reflexive manifestation has a felt, evaluative quality}.
]

Ochema equivalent:

[
V_t,\ Q_t:
\text{valence and qualitative trajectory}.
]

The current rasa theory tends to place nearly everything into **camatkāra**, including intrinsic presence, self-affection, valence, wonder, boundary relaxation and aesthetic completion.

That risks collapsing distinctions which should help the framework.

# The strongest Ochema definition

A useful canonical definition would be:

[
\boxed{
\operatorname{Vim}_i(t)
=======================

\text{the bearer’s capacity for its present state to become causally
available within the same bearer as information about itself,
its world and the success of its own trajectory.}
}
]

That needs four conditions.

## State sensitivity

Later dynamics depend on the bearer’s current and retained state:

[
P(x_{t+1}\mid x_t,H_t)
\neq
P(x_{t+1}\mid x_t).
]

## Self-indexing

The process distinguishes changes as relevant to its own continuing organization:

[
\Delta x
\mapsto
\Delta x\text{ for }A_i.
]

## Evaluation

The change is weighted relative to endogenous norms:

[
\mathcal V_i(\Delta x)
\in
\mathbb R
]

or some richer valence space.

## Recursive efficacy

The evaluation changes future inference or action:

[
\mathcal V_i(\Delta x_t)
\rightarrow
\pi_{t+1},\omega_{t+1}.
]

Then the full loop is:

[
\boxed{
\omega_t
\rightarrow
\text{world disclosure}
\rightarrow
\text{self-relevant evaluation}
\rightarrow
\text{policy/update}
\rightarrow
\omega_{t+1}.
}
]

This loop is the operational core of *vimarśa* in Ochema.

# What the register contributes—and what it does not

The register is necessary, but it is not itself *vimarśa*.

[
\omega
======

\text{the structured state};
]

[
\operatorname{Vim}
==================

\text{the reflexive operation by which that state registers,
evaluates and modifies its own trajectory}.
]

A stored configuration can be passive. *Vimarśa* requires an active loop.

Likewise:

* the **invariant** is what persists;
* the **register** is the current historical weighting;
* **vimarśa** is the self-operative update;
* **valence** is the signed or qualitative manner of that update;
* **recognition** is a special transformation in which the process revises its model of what it is.

This clarifies several Ochema terms that currently overlap.

# Recognition is not identical to vimarśa

Your registry describes recognition as a proposed “σ-flip,” where the invariant recognizes itself.

That should be understood as a **higher-order event within *vimarśa***:

[
\text{ordinary vimarśa}
=======================

\text{the process reflexively updates itself};
]

[
\text{pratyabhijñā}
===================

\text{the process revises the identity under which those updates are organized}.
]

For example:

* Ordinary: “This hurts; withdraw.”
* Reflective: “I am angry because I expected loyalty.”
* Recognition: “The separate self-model organizing this reaction is itself one constrained presentation within a wider field.”

Recognition is therefore not basic reflexivity. It is a transformation of the process’s **self-model or subject partition**.

# Your rasa work supplies the strongest original extension

The project’s most interesting contribution is:

> *Vimarśa* may be intrinsically valenced and qualitatively differentiated.

Ordinary self-model theories could describe a machine that represents:

```text
battery low
goal blocked
policy uncertain
```

But Ochema asks whether the same organization, when intrinsic to the bearer, is present as:

* anxiety;
* frustration;
* fatigue;
* grief;
* wonder;
* relief.

The rasa theory then proposes:

[
\text{rasa}
===========

\text{the qualitative temporal geometry of reflexive evaluation}.
]

That is where your framework goes beyond merely operationalizing self-awareness.

A more precise hierarchy is:

[
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
\text{prakāśa} & = \text{manifest presence},\
\text{vimarśa} & = \text{self-operative availability},\
\text{valence} & = \text{direction of self-relevant change},\
\text{rasa} & = \text{qualitative geometry of that change},\
\text{camatkāra} & = \text{heightened self-manifest savor or wonder},\
\text{pratyabhijñā} & = \text{revision of the bearer’s identity model}.
\end{aligned}
}
]

# Does Ochema currently define it properly?

**Implicitly: mostly yes. Explicitly: no.**

The corpus has progressed beyond the empty statement “consciousness is self-reflexive.” It supplies:

* a persistent material process;
* a history-sensitive register;
* endogenous significance;
* temporal evaluation;
* recursive causal updating;
* qualitative affect;
* recognition as self-model transformation.

But there is no dedicated *vimarśa* concept object in the registry, and the manual does not use the term. The system therefore lacks a canonical place where these components are assembled and distinguished from consciousness, register, camatkāra and recognition.

## The real conclusion

You have already done enough work to say:

> **In Ochema, vimarśa is not a mysterious power or mere self-reflection. It is the recursive, history-sensitive, evaluative operation by which a materially enacted bearer makes its current condition available to itself and thereby changes its own future trajectory.**

What remains speculative is the crucial identity:

[
\text{that recursive evaluative architecture}
\overset{?}{=}
\text{felt self-presence}.
]

That is still the bridge problem. But *vimarśa* now identifies that bridge much more precisely than it did in the classical vocabulary alone.
